WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.400
Here we describe the general process&nbsp;
of splicing and demonstrate how an open

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:18.480
reading frame is maintained within the mature&nbsp;
messenger RNA, or mRNA. In eukaryotes, mRNA is

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.560
transcribed from the five prime to three prime&nbsp;
direction using one strand of DNA as template.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.960
As the RNA is generated, it is processed&nbsp;
in order to give rise to a mature message.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:39.840
One key step of RNA processing is splicing. We&nbsp;
can use English letters, words, and sentences

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.320
to illustrate the key concepts of splicing. Let&nbsp;
us imagine that the following precursor message

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:53.120
has been generated. Let us also pretend that&nbsp;
each letter of this transcript is a nucleotide.

00:00:53.154 --> 00:01:02.963
As written, this message does not make much sense.&nbsp;
But if we highlight the exons using red letters, we can identify a

00:01:02.997 --> 00:01:09.069
short sentence when we read just&nbsp;
those letters. Conveniently for this illustration,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:16.640
all the exons are short, but do keep in mind&nbsp;
that exons and introns are of variable lengths.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:25.680
In real life, the minimal size for an intron is&nbsp;
about 40 nucleotides. Since the processed message is

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.880
a complete English sentence, the first six letters&nbsp;
of the precursor message highlighted in blue

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.920
before the definite article "the" corresponds to&nbsp;
the five prime Untranslated Region, or 5' UTR.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:50.077
The blue letters after the red letters end&nbsp;
correspond to the 3' UTR. The untranslated regions often contain

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:57.685
information that helps&nbsp;regulate translation. The black letters between the exons correspond to introns.

00:01:57.718 --> 00:02:05.626
An intron begins with&nbsp;a five prime splice donor site and ends with a three prime splice acceptor site.

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:12.232
Remember that the&nbsp;five prime splice donor site of the intron usually&nbsp;has the sequence GT,

00:02:12.266 --> 00:02:18.480
while the three prime splice&nbsp;
acceptor site has the sequence AG. When we remove

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:26.400
all the introns and begin reading at the first&nbsp;
word, the message tells us that "the fat old cat ate

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:34.000
the big rat." "The" is the beginning of our processed&nbsp;
message and represents the start codon of the open

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.800
reading frame. When you read this message, your&nbsp;
brain converts the message into a mental image.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:47.920
Similarly, the translational machinery reads the&nbsp;
processed transcript and converts it into a protein.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.160
Because an exon could end in the middle of&nbsp;
a word, we use the term "phase" to describe the

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:00.640
number of letters between the last complete word,&nbsp;
or codon, and the five prime splice donor site.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.720
If the last base of the codon coincides&nbsp;
with the end of an exon, then we would

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:14.480
consider the five prime splice donor site for&nbsp;
that exon to be in phase zero. In this example,

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:21.920
the word "fat" in our message is complete before&nbsp;
the splice donor site. Hence, we would consider

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:28.560
this splice donor site to be in Phase 0. If&nbsp;
the splice site cuts off the codon in an exon

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:35.120
after one base, we would consider the five prime&nbsp;
splice donor site for that exon to be in Phase 1.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:42.720
In this example the word "cat" is split&nbsp;
between two exons. The letter C is in

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:50.080
exon two and the letters A and T are in exon&nbsp;
three. If the splice site cuts off the codon in

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:56.480
an exon after two bases, we would consider the&nbsp;
five prime splice donor site for that exon to

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:05.280
be in Phase 2. In this example, the word "ate"&nbsp;
is split between two exons. The letters A and T

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:12.880
are in exon three and the letter E is in exon four.&nbsp;
Note that the phase of the five prime donor site

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.000
must be compatible with the phase of the&nbsp;
three prime acceptor site. The sum of the

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.680
donor and acceptor phases must either be zero,&nbsp;
no additional codon, or three (an additional codon).

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:33.840
Do remember to analyze splice junctions carefully.&nbsp;
Examine the splice junction between exons four and

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:42.160
five. There are two ways to form the words&nbsp;
"big rat." However, only the second option

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:55.280
utilizes the canonical five prime splice donor and&nbsp;
the three prime splice acceptor sites in intron four.

